# Seed corpus for the classical-text RAG (§5.12).
# These are GENERAL, widely-taught Jyotish principles stated in plain terms.
# They intentionally carry the honest reference "General principle" — NOT a verse
# number — so nothing here misrepresents a classical citation. Replace/augment
# with genuine public-domain translations (with real references) to get real
# shloka citations. See README.md for the format.
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Lagna (ascendant) and its lord describe the body, temperament, vitality and the overall direction of life; the strength and placement of the Lagna lord is a first measure of the native's resilience."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Moon signifies the mind and emotions. Its sign, nakshatra and the planets aspecting or joining it colour the native's mental disposition and receptivity."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A benefic in a kendra (1,4,7,10) or trikona (1,5,9) tends to protect and uplift the houses it influences; a malefic there can add force, ambition or friction depending on its dignity."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 10th house and its lord, together with the Sun, Saturn and Mercury, indicate profession, status and one's public role; the Dasamsa (D10) refines the reading of career."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Wealth is read from the 2nd (accumulated resources) and 11th (gains and fulfilment of desires) houses and their lords, with Jupiter and Venus as natural significators of prosperity."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Marriage and partnership are judged from the 7th house and its lord, from Venus, and — for depth — from the Navamsa (D9) and the Upapada Lagna (UL)."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 6th, 8th and 12th are the dusthana (difficult) houses; planets there often work through challenge, service, transformation or loss, and their results ripen especially in their dashas."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A retrograde planet is considered strong in motion (cheshta bala) but internalised in expression; a combust planet near the Sun loses visibility and independent effect."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A planet in its own sign or exaltation gives its results readily and auspiciously; in debilitation or an enemy's sign it must struggle to deliver, though neecha-bhanga (cancellation) can restore it."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Vimsottari dasha sequences the unfolding of results in time: the maha-dasha lord sets the theme of the period and the bhukti (sub-period) lord modifies it, read through the houses each lord rules and occupies."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Gochara (transit) results are judged from the natal Moon: certain houses from the Moon are favourable for each planet, but a favourable transit can be cancelled by vedha (an obstructing planet in a specific house)."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Sade Sati, Saturn's roughly seven-and-a-half year transit over the 12th, 1st and 2nd from the natal Moon, is a period of maturation and responsibility rather than mere misfortune; its effect depends on Saturn's natal dignity and the houses it rules."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 9th house, its lord and Jupiter indicate dharma, fortune, higher learning and the guiding values of life; a strong 9th supports grace, ethics and good counsel."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Raja yogas form when lords of kendras and trikonas associate; they indicate rises in status and authority, timed to the dashas of the planets involved."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Arudha Lagna (AL) shows how the native is perceived by the world — image, reputation and material manifestation — as distinct from the Lagna, which shows the actual self."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 3rd house governs courage, initiative, siblings (especially younger), short journeys and the hands; it is an upachaya house, so difficulties there tend to reduce with effort and age."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 4th house governs the mother, the home, land and vehicles, formal education and — as the seat of sukha — inner contentment; the Moon and Mercury are its natural significators."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 5th house governs children, intelligence, purva punya (merit carried forward), speculation and mantra; Jupiter is its natural significator, and the 5th lord's dignity colours how easily its results arrive."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 8th house governs longevity, sudden events, inheritance and other people's resources, and occult or research work; planets there tend to give results through crisis, delay or transformation rather than smoothly."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 11th house governs gains, elder siblings, networks and the fulfilment of desires; it is an upachaya house and generally improves over time, but as the 12th from the 12th it can also drain what the 12th signifies."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 12th house governs loss, expenditure, foreign residence, seclusion, sleep and moksha; a benefic there often turns expenditure into investment, charity or spiritual practice rather than waste."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 2nd house governs accumulated wealth, family, speech and diet; the 2nd and 7th lords are the natural maraka (death-inflicting) lords, whose periods commonly bring transitions rather than only endings."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The 6th house governs illness, debt, enemies, service and daily discipline; a strong 6th lord can indicate the capacity to defeat opposition, which is why some malefic placements there behave constructively."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A planet in the same sign in both the rasi chart and the navamsa is vargottama and behaves as though strengthened — its results are more consistent and less easily disturbed."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Combustion (asta) occurs when a planet is close to the Sun in longitude; the planet's outward expression is obscured, though its inner significations may intensify. Mercury and Venus are combust often, so the condition alone is not a verdict."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Digbala (directional strength) is gained by Jupiter and Mercury in the 1st, the Moon and Venus in the 4th, Saturn in the 7th, and the Sun and Mars in the 10th; a planet with digbala expresses its nature more freely."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Shadbala assembles six kinds of strength — positional, directional, temporal, motional, natural and aspectual — so a planet may be weak by sign and still strong overall. No single measure decides a planet's capacity."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The natural benefics are Jupiter, Venus, waxing Moon and unafflicted Mercury; the natural malefics are Saturn, Mars, the Sun, the nodes and a Mercury joined to malefics. Functional benefic status depends on which houses a planet rules from the lagna."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A planet that rules both a kendra and a trikona from the lagna is a yogakaraka and is among the most reliable sources of good in a chart; for Taurus and Libra lagnas Saturn takes that role, and for Cancer and Leo lagnas Mars does."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Planetary friendship has two layers: the fixed natural friendships, and the temporary friendship that arises from a planet's position relative to another in the chart. The two combine into the compound relationship actually used in judgement."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Avasthas describe the state a planet is in — its dignity, its age within the sign, and its mood (baladi, jagradadi, deeptadi). They modulate how much of a planet's promised result actually reaches the native."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Each nakshatra spans 13 degrees 20 minutes and carries a ruling planet, a deity and a temperament; the nakshatra of the Moon at birth (janma nakshatra) sets the Vimsottari dasha sequence and much of the native's instinctive nature."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Tara Bala measures the count from the janma nakshatra to the nakshatra of the day: the 3rd, 5th and 7th taras (vipat, pratyak, naidhana) are considered difficult, and the 2nd, 4th, 6th, 8th and 9th supportive."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Chandra Bala measures the transiting Moon's house from the natal Moon; the 1st, 3rd, 6th, 7th, 10th and 11th are generally favourable, and the 4th, 8th and 12th are avoided for beginnings."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Paksha bala: the Moon grows in strength from new to full and wanes thereafter, so a chart born near the full Moon carries a stronger lunar significator than one born near the new Moon."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The navamsa (D9) shows the inner strength of every planet and is read for marriage and for dharma; a planet dignified in the rasi chart but fallen in the navamsa promises more than it delivers."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The dasamsa (D10) is read for career and public standing, the chaturthamsa (D4) for property and fixed assets, the saptamsa (D7) for children, and the trimsamsa (D30) for misfortunes and moral temperament."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A divisional chart is judged from its own lagna, but its verdict never overrides the rasi chart — the vargas refine what the rasi chart has already promised rather than replacing it."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Ashtakavarga scores each sign by the benefic points contributed by the seven planets and the lagna; a transit through a sign carrying many bindus tends to deliver more of what that transit promises."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "In the sarvashtakavarga a sign holding fewer than about 25 points is considered weak and one holding more than about 30 strong; the comparison between signs matters more than any single total."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Within a mahadasha the antardasha lord decides the immediate texture of events, and the relationship between the two lords — friendship, mutual house position, shared house rulership — decides whether the period reads as support or friction."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A dasha lord delivers the results of the houses it rules, the house it occupies, the planets it joins and aspects, and its own natural significations; these are weighed together rather than read one at a time."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The dasha of a planet in a dusthana, or of a planet closely afflicted, tends to bring the affairs of that planet through obstruction; the same planet may still give results if it is otherwise strong."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A transit only ripens what the natal chart and the running dasha already allow. A favourable transit during an unsupportive dasha gives a smaller result than the same transit during a supportive one."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Jupiter's transit over the natal Moon's 2nd, 5th, 7, 9th and 11th houses is traditionally favourable, and over the 4th, 8th and 12th less so; its roughly one-year stay in a sign makes it the transit most easily felt as a change of climate."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A retrograde transiting planet revisits a degree three times — direct, retrograde, then direct again — and matters attached to that degree characteristically return for revision rather than concluding on the first pass."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Vedha (obstruction) in gochara: a favourable transit result can be cancelled by another planet occupying the sign that obstructs it, which is why transits are read as a set rather than one planet at a time."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Sade Sati has three phases as Saturn crosses the 12th, 1st and 2nd from the natal Moon; the phases are felt differently — the first as loss or withdrawal, the second as pressure on the self, the third as strain on resources and family."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The small panoti, Saturn's transit of the 4th and 8th from the natal Moon (ashtama shani and kantaka shani), is considered testing in a similar way to Sade Sati though shorter."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A Gaja Kesari yoga forms when Jupiter is in a kendra from the Moon; it indicates good judgement and standing, though like every yoga its result depends on the dignity of the planets that form it."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Pancha Mahapurusha yogas form when Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus or Saturn occupies its own or exaltation sign in a kendra from the lagna, each giving the character of that planet in a marked degree."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Neecha bhanga (cancellation of debilitation) is considered when the lord of the debilitation sign, or the planet exalted in that sign, is in a kendra from the lagna or the Moon; the planet then recovers much of its capacity."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Kemadruma yoga is described when no planet occupies the 2nd or 12th from the Moon; it is very commonly cancelled, so it should not be reported without checking the cancellations."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Mangal (Kuja) dosha is assessed from Mars in the 1st, 2nd, 4th, 7th, 8th or 12th house, counted from the lagna, the Moon and Venus; matching two charts requires comparing the dosha on both sides rather than judging one alone."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The chara karakas are assigned by planetary longitude in descending order: the highest is the Atmakaraka, which signifies the self and the direction of the soul's work in this life."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Karakamsa is the navamsa sign of the Atmakaraka placed in the rasi chart; the houses counted from it describe the native's inner life and spiritual inclination."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Argala is the intervention one house exercises over another — from the 2nd, 4th and 11th primarily — and is cancelled by virodha argala from the 12th, 10th and 3rd respectively."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The Panchanga has five limbs — tithi, vaara, nakshatra, yoga and karana — and an electional judgement weighs all five together with the rising sign of the moment."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Abhijit muhurta, the eighth of the fifteen day-muhurtas around local noon, is generally auspicious for beginnings; Rahu kala, Yamaganda and Gulika kala are avoided."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A muhurta is only as good as it is for the person acting: the same window is read again through the querent's janma nakshatra (Tara Bala), natal Moon (Chandra Bala) and running dasha before it is called favourable for them."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Lagna shuddhi — the purity of the rising sign at the chosen moment — is a primary test in muhurta: the lagna lord should be strong and the 8th house free of affliction."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Gulika and Mandi are shadowy points derived from the eighth part of the day or night ruled by Saturn; their contact with a house or lord is read as a source of obstruction."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "The badhaka house is the 11th for movable signs, the 9th for fixed and the 7th for dual; its lord is a specific source of obstruction in the affairs it touches."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Bhava madhya (the cusp) is the strongest point of a house; a planet close to the cusp acts on that house most fully, and one near the sandhi (junction) between houses acts weakly on either."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "In the KP method a planet gives the results of its nakshatra lord (star lord) more than its own, and the sub-lord decides whether the matter fructifies; significators are gathered before any timing is attempted."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "A single factor never decides a reading. A conclusion is only reported when the house, its lord, the natural significator and the running period agree; where they disagree, the disagreement itself is the finding."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Remedial measures in the classical tradition are conduct, charity, mantra, fasting and the honouring of the deity of the afflicted planet; they are described as moderating a result, never as removing what the chart has promised."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Longevity is treated in the classics as the first question, because the results of every other house are contingent on it; and the texts themselves caution that its determination is difficult and not to be pronounced lightly."}
{"source": "General Jyotish principle", "reference": "General principle", "text": "Where the classics give a stark verdict on birth, marriage or death, the tradition also supplies extensive cancellations. A rule quoted without its cancellations misrepresents the text it comes from."}
